# Four requests that cannot mean anything, and the four reasons.
#
#   omn validate -f examples/sched-refusals.yml.rejected --input-format yaml
#   omn -f examples/sched-refusals.yml.rejected --input-format yaml ; echo $?  # 2
#
# The extension is `.yml.rejected`, and that is not decoration. Every session in
# `examples/` that ends in a format's extension is required to validate --
# `tests/cli.rs::the_shipped_examples_validate` walks the directory so that no
# example can ship broken by being left off a list. A session whose whole point
# is to be rejected cannot satisfy that, and should not: it is not broken, it is
# the class D scenario. Renaming it out of the walk is the smallest honest way
# to hold both, and `--input-format yaml` is what a file with no recognised
# extension needs. The better fix is two lines in that shared test, and belongs
# to whoever owns it.
#
# A rejection is not a failure and not a missing feature: it is a request the
# catalog cannot make sense of, and the exit code says which of the three it is.
#
#   2  a rejection          the request cannot mean anything
#   1  no viable backend    this machine cannot serve it
#   3  no collector         this build does not implement it
#
# The other two are in examples/sched-not-implemented.yml (exit 3) and, for this
# family, nowhere: every `sched` backend this machine could lack is available
# here, so exit 1 has no honest example. What does produce exit 1 here is
# `omn sched.switches --mode count`, and that is a build gap wearing a machine
# gap's exit code -- see docs/scenarios/sched.md under "Defects found".
#
# Each request below must be refused by name. A refusal that sends a reader to
# the wrong flag costs more than no message at all.
name: four reasons to say no
requests:
  # `sched.wakeup` reports one event per wakeup and a wakeup has no duration,
  # so there is no distribution to bucket.
  - label: an-emit-the-source-does-not-declare
    source: sched.wakeup
    target: {launch: ["/bin/true"]}
    mode: trace
    emit: [hist]

  # A block has a beginning and an end, and a sample that catches one without
  # the other has no duration to report.
  - label: a-mode-the-source-does-not-declare
    source: sched.offcpu
    target: {launch: ["/bin/true"]}
    mode: count
    emit: [summary]

  # `sched.offcpu` filters on comm, pid and state. A block belongs to a task,
  # not to a cpu: the cpu it left is not the cpu it comes back on.
  - label: a-field-the-source-cannot-filter-on
    source: sched.offcpu
    target: {launch: ["/bin/true"]}
    mode: trace
    emit: [summary]
    filter: "cpu == 3"

  # Blocked time is attributed per task, and an interface names none.
  - label: a-target-the-source-cannot-observe
    source: sched.offcpu
    target: {iface: "lo"}
    mode: trace
    emit: [summary]
